Close a continued rule's m4 quote after it has been opened

A rule that borrows the next rule's action with `|' makes flex stop with

    flex: ERROR: end of file in string

for every back end that reaches its output through m4 hooks: --emit=c99,
--emit=go, and any skeleton written against the hooks. --emit=c (the
C/C++ skeleton that predates them) is unaffected and still emits the
fall-through case correctly.

    %option noyywrap
    %%
    "a"     |
    "b"     return 1;

The two halves of the quoting disagree about order. finish_rule() in
nfa.c ends every rule by opening a quote for the action that follows:

    line_directive_out(NULL, infilename, linenum);
    add_action("[[");

and scan.l's `|' arm closes it:

    add_action("]""]");
    continued_action = true;
    ++linenum;
    return '\n';

but the arm runs in the scanner, and finish_rule() runs when the parser
reduces the rule, which is after the arm has returned '\n'. So the close
is written before the open exists. Counting over the m4 source of the
grammar above gives equal numbers of [[ and ]] - the totals balance - but
the depth ends at 1, with a stray ]] where the first rule should have
begun.

The fix is to ask for the close and let finish_rule() write it once the
quote is there.

<<EOF>> takes the other order. build_eof_action() opens the quote for the
EOF rule's action, and the parser has already reduced EOF_OP by the time
the scanner reads the `|', so there the quote does exist and the close
belongs in the arm:

    %option noyywrap
    %%
    <<EOF>>   |
    [a-z]+    return yytext;

Deferring it there as well leaves build_eof_action()'s quote open, the
following rule's case arm inside it - so M4_HOOK_NORMAL_STATE_CASE_ARM
reaches the output unexpanded, and the deferred ]] lands in it as text.
Released flex takes this grammar and emits the fall-through case
correctly. in_eof_action tells the two orders apart, and clearing it
there is also what tells the next rule's action that it is a rule's own
and not the EOF rule's.

Introduced in two steps. The quoting pair arrived in 4bffa41 ("Improved
M4 quotation", 2016-09-27), which is in v2.6.2 and later, so the
inversion is present in released flex; it is harmless there because no
released back end reads the action through m4 hooks. It becomes fatal
with the hook-based skeletons, which start at 978a4a3 ("Begin work on a
C99 front end", 2020-10-16) and 079b2d1 ("Eliminate YY_BREAK macro,
replaced with an m4 hook", 2020-10-28). Neither is in any tag, so this
affects master rather than a release.

Applies to flex master as of 4fcc714.
---
--- a/src/flexdef.h
+++ b/src/flexdef.h
@@ -458,6 +458,11 @@
 extern bool syntaxerror, eofseen;
 extern int yymore_used, reject, real_reject, continued_action, in_rule;
 
+/* Whether a rule continued with `|' is still waiting for the quote that
+ * finish_rule() opens, so that it can be closed in the right order.
+ */
+extern int continued_action_pending;
+
 /* Variables used in the flex input routines:
  * datapos - characters on current output line
  * dataline - number of contiguous lines of data in current data
--- a/src/main.c
+++ b/src/main.c
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@
 /* these globals are all defined and commented in flexdef.h */
 bool    syntaxerror, eofseen;
 int     yymore_used, reject, real_reject, continued_action, in_rule;
+int     continued_action_pending;
 int     datapos, dataline, linenum;
 FILE   *skelfile = NULL;
 int     skel_ind = 0;
--- a/src/nfa.c
+++ b/src/nfa.c
@@ -286,6 +286,15 @@
 
 	line_directive_out(NULL, infilename, linenum);
         add_action("[[");
+
+	/* A rule continued with `|' has no action of its own, so the quote just
+	 * opened is closed straight away.  Doing it where the `|' was read would
+	 * close a quote that did not exist yet.
+	 */
+	if (continued_action_pending) {
+		add_action("]""]");
+		continued_action_pending = false;
+	}
 }
 
 
--- a/src/scan.l
+++ b/src/scan.l
@@ -576,7 +576,25 @@
                             yyless(amt);
                         }
                         else {
-                            add_action("]""]");
+                            /* The quote this closes is opened by finish_rule(),
+                             * which the parser has not reached yet, so ask for
+                             * the close rather than writing it here.
+                             */
+                            /* Whether there is a quote to close yet depends
+                             * on which ran first.  For a normal rule the
+                             * parser has not reached finish_rule, so ask it
+                             * to close one it has not opened.  After <<EOF>>
+                             * build_eof_action has already opened one and the
+                             * close belongs here, and clearing in_eof_action
+                             * is what tells the next rule's action that it is
+                             * a rule's and not the EOF rule's.
+                             */
+                            if (in_eof_action) {
+                                add_action("]""]");
+                                in_eof_action = false;
+                            }
+                            else
+                                continued_action_pending = true;
                             continued_action = true;
                             ++linenum;
                             return '\n';
